after cloning the repo 
install dependences
The admin code provides an API for creating and managing administrator accounts.

adminModel.js defines the admin record: name, email, password, and role. It hashes passwords before saving and provides a way to check passwords during login.
adminController.js implements the actions: sign up, log in, list or view admins, update or delete an account, and return the current admin. It omits passwords from responses and issues a JWT after signup or login.
adminRoutes.js maps those actions to endpoints under /api/admin.
auth.middleware.js checks the bearer token and loads the admin for protected routes. It also restricts deletion to admins whose role is founder.
app.js mounts the routes in the Express app.
The main flow is: an admin signs in, receives a token, then sends that token to access protected admin-management endpoints. One important detail: /api/admin/signup is public, and signup accepts role: "founder", so anyone could create a founder account through that endpoint.


Menu module

The menu code provides an API for restaurant owners to manage their menu, and for customers to view it.

Category.js defines a menu section: restaurant and name. A unique index stops one restaurant having two categories with the same name.
MenuItem.js defines a food item: restaurant, category, name, description, price (whole number, 0 or more), imageUrl and isAvailable.
menu.validator.js checks input before anything touches the database. It checks ids, name length, price, image link and true/false values, and returns all the errors in one 400 response.
menu.controller.js implements the actions: view menu, create, rename or delete a category, and create, update, set availability or delete an item. It passes the request to the service and replies with {success, data, message}.
menu.service.js holds the business rules. It checks that the logged-in owner owns the restaurant, that a category belongs to the same restaurant as its item, and that a category with items can't be deleted. Duplicate category names return 409. The public menu is built here, grouped by category.
menu.routes.js maps these actions to 8 endpoints. Owner routes are protected by auth and allowRoles('owner'), and the public menu route is open.
seed.js fills the database with a standard restaurant menu for testing.
menu.validator.test.js has 8 automated tests for the validators.

The main flow is: an owner logs in and receives a token, then sends it to create categories and items for their own restaurant. A customer calls the public menu endpoint and gets the approved restaurant's menu grouped by category.

Endpoints:
GET /restaurants/:id/menu (public)
POST /restaurants/:id/categories (owner)
PUT /categories/:id (owner)
DELETE /categories/:id (owner)
POST /restaurants/:id/menu-items (owner)
PUT /menu-items/:id (owner)
PATCH /menu-items/:id/availability (owner)
DELETE /menu-items/:id (owner)

How it was tested: 8 automated tests run with node --test test/menu.validator.test.js. All 8 endpoints were also tested by hand against the real MongoDB Atlas database with curl and a Postman collection, covering 400, 401, 403, 404 and 409 responses.

One important detail: the menu routes are not mounted in app.js yet. They need the Auth middleware (auth and allowRoles) and the Restaurant model, which are still to be merged. Until then I tested with temporary stand-ins that are not in the repository. Ownership is enforced in the service, so one owner can't edit another owner's menu.
